      {
        "delta": {
          "content": "The Alps are the highest and most extensive mountain range system that lies entirely within Europe, stretching approximately 1,200 kilometres (750 miles) across eight countries: Austria, France, Germany, Italy, Liechtenstein, Monaco, Slovenia, and Switzerland. They form a crescent-shaped geographic feature of central Europe, ranging in an 800 km (500 mi) arc from east to west and up to 200 km (120 mi) wide. The mean height of the mountain peaks is about 2.5 km (1.6 mi), and the highest peak is Mont Blanc, at 4,810 meters (15,781 feet), located on the French-Italian border. The Alps were formed over tens of millions of years due to the collision of the African and Eurasian tectonic plates, resulting in the rise of marine sedimentary rocks into high mountain peaks such as Mont Blanc and the Matterhorn. The region spans roughly from the Mediterranean Sea through central Europe to the Adriatic Sea and has significant influence and history, including its role as a source of minerals and tourism destination today. The countries with the greatest alpine territory are Switzerland, France, Austria, and Italy <[2]> <[7]> <[10]>.",
